Create an SVG diagram for a technical talk: three virtualization stacks side by
side, same deck style as the other figures (1200x675, white background,
'IBM Plex Sans' / 'Source Code Pro', title in #8a0404, green = VM,
blue = VMM/user space, purple = hypervisor/kernel, grey = hardware).

Each of the three boxes shows the same layering: VMs on top, VMM processes
below them, the hypervisor in the kernel, hardware at the bottom.

- Box 1: Linux/KVM as the hypervisor, with several VMMs side by side on top of
  it - Cloud Hypervisor, QEMU and VirtualBox (KVM backend) - each running its
  own VM, with faded "ghost" VMs indicating that many VMs run side by side.
- Box 2: VirtualBox with its own hypervisor, the vboxdrv kernel module on
  Linux, with its VMM processes on top.
- Box 3: VMware (Workstation) with its own hypervisor, the vmmon kernel
  module; note that ESXi instead ships its own OS for bare metal.

Footer: with KVM the hypervisor is shared and the VMM is just a program, while
VirtualBox and VMware ship both halves themselves.

Emphasis: the VMM row and the hypervisor row are highlighted (stronger fill and
border); everything else (column frames and headings, VMs, hardware, per-column
notes) is not blurred but drawn lighter (reduced opacity) so it steps back.
